:1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"Key point 1 is covered. The answer says that when underlying holdings are costly or hard to trade (wide spreads, low depth, closures, volatility), market makers' hedging costs and uncertainty rise and they may quote wider. It also notes that liquid holdings and hedges support competitive quotes. Key point 2 is covered. Hedge execution costs, basis risk, and creation/redemption frictions (basket trading, financing, fees, operational constraints, inventory risk) are said to 'feed into quotes'. This matches the idea that market-maker costs are recovered through the spread, though the answer never says outright that market makers earn the spread. Key point 3 is substantively covered. The answer says historical volume reflects only past ETF trading, not current order-book depth, holdings liquidity, or dealers' ability to hedge and 'process baskets'. It adds that a lightly traded ETF can still have tight quotes and high volume does not guarantee them. The point that creation/redemption lets dealers adjust inventory beyond existing ETF shares is implied through basket processing rather than stated explicitly, but this is a minor gap. Extra drivers (volatility, market closures, correlated-instrument hedging) are sound and allowed. There is no ranking, no universal spread level, and no numerical claim.\"}","ttft_ms":2841,"type":"result","duration_ms":6786,"uuid":"cdcb95cd-0e15-435b-b598-1fc599b378b1","ttft_stream_ms":570,"time_to_request_ms":70,"first_content_frame_ms":570,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
